 "literal": "Bamboo-shouldered young girl making a doll / surrounding the reed-grass pond, and she / beautiful breasts rising with fullness, painted to obtain beauty / cosmetic patterns, those who guard her do not even know / In the righteous sceptre court of the just king / if I ask for my right, how will it become? / Greatly foolish, indeed / is this bustling town, what a pity!",
 "translation": "The young girl with bamboo-like shoulders made a doll and played around the pond of reed-grass. Her guards do not even notice the cosmetic patterns painted to beautify her full, rising breasts. If I were to demand my right in the court of a just king with a righteous sceptre, what would happen? This bustling town is indeed very foolish, what a pity!",
 "modern": "மூங்கில் போன்ற தோள்களைக் கொண்ட இளம்பெண், பஞ்சாய்ப் புற்கள் நிறைந்த குளக்கரையில் பாவை செய்து விளையாடினாள். அவளுடைய செழுமையான மார்பகங்களில் அழகு பெற எழுதப்பட்ட தொய்யில் ஓவியங்களை அவளைக் காப்பவர்கள் கவனிக்கவே இல்லை. முறைமை உடைய அரசனது செங்கோல் அவையத்தில் நான் என் உரிமையைக் கேட்டால் என்னவாகும்? இந்த ஆரவாரமிக்க ஊர் மிகவும் அறிவற்றது, பரிதாபத்திற்குரியது!",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the hero in a Kuṟiñci (mountainous) landscape. He has secretly made love to the heroine, and as a sign of their union, he has painted beautiful cosmetic patterns (தொய்யில்) on her breasts. However, her foster-mother and other guards, who watch over her constantly, fail to notice these patterns, remaining completely ignorant of her secret love. The hero compares this situation to a citizen seeking justice in a king's court; if he were to openly declare his love and claim his right to marry her, the ignorant townspeople would not understand. He laments the foolishness of the bustling town (அழுங்கல் ஊர்) which remains blind to what is happening right in front of them.",
